Let MZ_ASSERT be overridden, and add MINIZ_NO_ASSERT.

MZ_ASSERT expands to assert(), and 26 of its call sites in miniz.c are
reachable from malformed input. Because it is defined unconditionally there
is no way to override it from the command line, so what it does is decided
entirely by NDEBUG.

R supplies -DNDEBUG, so ordinary and CRAN builds compile every one of them
away -- but a -UNDEBUG build, which is what devtools' debug install uses,
turns a malformed byte sequence into abort() and takes the whole R session
with it instead of raising a condition. That is the last place in zukomp
where hostile input can terminate the process rather than return an error.

MINIZ_NO_ASSERT makes the shipped behaviour explicit and identical in both
kinds of build, rather than leaving it to whether NDEBUG happens to be set.
It removes no checking that a release build was doing: with NDEBUG these
call sites are already no-ops, so this only stops a debug build from
diverging from the one that ships.

The guard is an opt-out and upstream behaviour is unchanged unless
MINIZ_NO_ASSERT is defined, so it is written to be upstreamable as-is. The
#ifndef also lets an embedder substitute something that records a status
rather than discarding it.

--- a/miniz.h
+++ b/miniz.h
@@ -662,7 +662,15 @@
 #define MZ_TIME_T time_t
 #endif
 
+#ifndef MZ_ASSERT
+#ifdef MINIZ_NO_ASSERT
+/* Defined to nothing rather than removed: the call sites are statements,
+   and several are the only use of the variable they test. */
+#define MZ_ASSERT(x) ((void)0)
+#else
 #define MZ_ASSERT(x) assert(x)
+#endif
+#endif
 
 #ifdef MINIZ_NO_MALLOC
 #define MZ_MALLOC(x) NULL
